  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale  \nThe Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem seeks a step function h(x) \u2208 [0,1] over [0,2] with \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1, minimizing the maximum overlap integral C\u2085. Conventional approaches like PSO may struggle due to non-convexity and high dimensionality. This plan introduces a novel convex relaxation via Lagrangian duality, enabling exact constraint handling and gradient-based optimization with projected updates. The dual problem ensures feasibility while optimizing the objective, balancing the trade-off between overlap minimization and constraint satisfaction.\n\n2. Mathematical specification  \nWe define h:\u211d\u2192[0,1] as a piecewise constant function represented by samples h_i at x_i = 2i/n_points. Let dx = 2.0/n_points. The discrete overlap is:\n$$\nC_5 = \\max_{k\u2208[-n_points+1, n_points-1]} \\left( \\sum_{i=0}^{n_points-1} h_i (1 - h_{(i+k) \\mod n_points}) \\cdot dx \\right)\n$$\nsubject to:\n1. 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 for all i\n2. \u2211h_i = n_points/2\n\nThe dual formulation is derived via Lagrangian multiplier method, introducing \u03bb for the overlap constraints and \u03bc for magnitude bounds. The dual objective balances violation costs for each constraint.\n\n3. Implementation procedure  \na) Initialize h from initial_h_values or uniform distribution.\nb) Define Lagrangian dual problem with multipliers \u03bb and \u03bc.\nc) Solve dual problem using CVXPY to find optimal \u03bc and constraint violations.\nd) Compute gradient updates for h: \u2207h = (1 - 2h) \u2299 (\u03c4 - \u03bb * (1 - h))\ne) Project h to feasible region:\n   i) Clip h \u2208 [0,1]\n   ii) Normalize to \u2211h = n_points/2\nf) Solve dual at updated h and repeat until budget exhausted.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations  \n- n_points: 70 (matches current resolution for compatibility).\n- Learning rate: adaptive based on dual problem resolution.\n- Initial weights: Start with \u03bc=10, \u03bb=5. Adjust based on constraint tightness.\n- Use exact constraint projection to maintain feasibility throughout.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness  \n- Time complexity: O(budget_s * n_points^2) due to nested dual solves.\n- Robustness: Feasibility projection ensures valid h at all steps.\n- Early termination checks at each iteration.\n\n6. Validation and return contract  \n- Verify feasibility (h \u2208 [0,1] and integral=1).\n- Compute exact C\u2085 using evaluator for final h.\n- Ensure C\u2085 \u2264 0.38085 as required.",
